  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThrough Thursday night:\n\nOther than a few spits of drizzle near Lake Michigan, most of \nthe area has since dried out from the overnight and early \nmorning showers and thunderstorms. The combination of overcast \nskies and persistent onshore northeasterly winds have held \ntemperatures in the low to mid 60s near/north of I-80. Farther \nsouth, a thinning of the stratus layer with occasional peeks of\nsunshine has allowed temperatures to warm into the upper 60s \nand lower 70s. Generally expect the cloud cover to fill back in \nthrough this evening and overnight with temperatures only \ndropping into the 50s.\n\nA fall storm system remains on track to lift across the region \non Thursday which will bring widespread showers and a few \nrumbles of thunder to the area. The greatest shower coverage is \ncurrently favored late morning through the afternoon. Hi-res \nguidance continues to depict a relative minimum in precipitation \ndeveloping on the southwest side of the surface low during the \nlate afternoon and early evening. Depending on how far north the\nlow tracks, increasing instability southeast of this dry wedge \nmay lift into the far southern forecast area, south of a Pontiac\nto Rensselaer line. While the better instability remains \nfarther south into central and southern Illinois, an isolated \nstronger storm can't be ruled out. Any more robust showers and \nembedded thunderstorms will be capable of efficient rainfall \nrates for this time of year in the presence of PWATs \npotentially upwards of 1.5-2\". The limited coverage of stronger \nshowers/storms should keep any flooding potential tied mainly to\nponding on roadways and poor drainage spots.\n\n\nFriday:\n\nIn the wake of the departing storm system and associated strong\ncold front, the surface pressure gradient increases markedly \nacross the area on Friday. This will lead to blustery northwest \nwinds gusting up to 30-40+ mph, highest along the northwest \nIndiana lakeshore. In addition, model guidance has trended \nslightly more saturated within the low-cloud layer which may \nsupport the redevelopment of gusty diurnal showers. Weak \ninstability within the layer and colder cloud tops may support \nsome graupel as well. Temperatures on Friday will likely \nstruggle to warm out of the low to mid 50s, making for the \ncoldest day of the season so far.\n\nAs the northwesterly winds begin to turn more northerly over \nLake Michigan, a broken band (or bands) of lake effect rain \nshowers will focus into northwest Indiana late Friday afternoon \nthrough the evening. Waves are also expected to increase and may\nlead to minor lakeshore flooding or erosion, particularly along\nthe Porter County shore.\n\n\nFriday night onward:\n\nSkies will quickly clear out after midnight Friday, which will \nallow for efficient radiational cooling amidst an already cooler\nairmass. This will likely lead to areas of frost development \noutside of Chicago and away from Lake Michigan, coverage \ndependent upon how light winds end up. Can't rule out \ntemperatures in portions of interior northern Illinois dropping \ninto the lower 30s. However, confidence is not high enough for a\nfreeze watch with this update.\n\nWhile still cooler on Saturday with temperatures in the 50s to \naround 60, a return of southwesterly low-level flow will begin a\ngradual warming trend that continues through early next week, \nbringing a return to above normal temperatures.\n\nPetr",
